libc for bcc09

bcc09's C library, ported from dev86's, with the parts dev86 didn't
have written for bcc09.  It has:

  include/  headers: ctype.h, dirent.h, errno.h, fcntl.h, limits.h,
            malloc.h, math.h, stdarg.h, stdbool.h, stddef.h, stdint.h,
            stdio.h, stdlib.h, string.h, unistd.h, sys/stat.h,
            sys/types.h, features.h; and the targets' coco.h and
            vectrex.h
            (stdint.h: no 64-bit types; stdbool.h: cc1's _Bool)
  bcc/      run-time helpers that the compiler calls for int and long
            operations the 6809 can't do in a few instructions, and
            for floating point (fp*.s)
  ctype/    ctype.h's table and toupper and tolower
  malloc/   malloc, free, calloc and realloc (dev86's)
  stdio/    stdio.h: the FILE functions (stdio.c, dev86's) and the
            printf and scanf families (printf.c and scanf.c, written
            for bcc09), with floating point in fpfmt.c and scanfp.c;
            and POSIX's getline and getdelim (getdelim.c), which read
            a line of any length into a buffer from malloc
  stdlib/   stdlib.h's other functions: atoi, atol, strtol, strtoul,
            strtod, atof, abs, labs, qsort, bsearch, rand, srand,
            atexit, exit and _exit, and dev86's itoa, ltoa, ultoa,
            ltostr and ultostr; errno; sbrk and brk, for malloc; and big
            integers (bignum.c) for strtod and printf's %f
  math/     math.h's functions, from Sun's fdlibm (see math/README)
  string/   string.h's functions; memcpy (which the compiler also calls
            to copy structures) is in assembly, and the rest are C,
            compiled by bcc09
  sa/       the default target (see below)
  cb/       the Color BASIC target (see below)
  rom/      the ROM cartridge target (see below), with cb/'s I/O
  vec/      the Vectrex target (see below)
  sim/      the sim6809 target (see below)
  os9/      the OS-9 target (see below); os9obj/ holds all of libc
            built again for it (made by "make")
  flex/     the FLEX target (see below)

"make" builds libc.a, and each target's start-up code and library, with
this tree's bcc09, as09 and ar09 (libc/.bcc holds links that bcc09 uses
to find this tree's passes); "make install" puts them in
$(PREFIX)/share/bcc09, where bcc09 tells ld09 to look for them, and the
headers in $(PREFIX)/share/bcc09/include, where bcc09 looks for them.

All of libc is under the GNU Library General Public License, version 2
(see COPYING.LIB), rather than the GPL that covers the rest of bcc09,
since it is linked into the programs bcc09 builds.  Most of the C sources
come from dev86's libc (some of them from the GNU C Library); the files
that say so were written for bcc09, either because dev86's had no
license (strcspn, qsort, bsearch, itoa) or to fix them (strtol, rand,
exit, atexit), as were the assembly sources.  Some sources hold several
functions, which the Makefile compiles one at a time with -DL_name, so
that a program links only what it uses.

Changes from dev86:
  - strncat called memchr with its arguments in the wrong order, so it
    copied n bytes whatever the source's length; memcmp gave results of
    the wrong sign; strcasecmp and strncasecmp returned the difference of
    the original characters, which can have the wrong sign ("a", "B")
  - strchr, strrchr, memchr and memccpy convert c to a char
  - strtol and strtoul accepted a digit equal to the base, didn't set
    endptr back to the start when there were no digits, read "0x" as a
    prefix with no hex digit after it, and ignored overflow: they now
    return LONG_MAX, LONG_MIN or ULONG_MAX and set errno to ERANGE
  - itoa got INT_MIN wrong; atoi and atol skip white space as isspace()
    defines it
  - isprint and isgraph were true for EOF and characters over 0x7F, and
    the toupper and tolower macros evaluated their argument twice (they
    are functions now)
  - rand is the C standard's example generator
  - malloc grows the heap 256 bytes at a time rather than 4K; calloc
    fails if elm * sz doesn't fit in a size_t, rather than allocating
    too little; there is no alloca (its comment says it should probably
    be considered GPL code)
  - size_t is unsigned (memmove relies on that); string.h no longer
    defines strxfrm as strncpy, which returns the wrong type
  - stdio: fclose unlinked a FILE from the list of open ones badly,
    losing the ones before it (so they weren't flushed at exit);
    ungetc(EOF) pushed back a byte, and ungetc didn't clear the end of
    file indicator; fgets with a count of 0 overran its buffer; BUFSIZ
    is 256; exit() flushed the streams before the atexit() functions
    registered before stdio was first used, losing their output (stdio
    registered its flush with atexit() then; exit() now flushes after
    every atexit() function, through __stdio_exit)
  - the printf and scanf families are new: dev86's printf.c and
    scanf.c had no license, and its printf departed from the C standard
    (precision on integers, %X, # and %p); see stdio/printf.c and
    stdio/scanf.c
What C89 has that libc doesn't yet: time.h, tmpfile, tmpnam, and
rename but on FLEX.  The rest of C89 is written for bcc09:
assert.h (stdlib/assert.c), setjmp.h (stdlib/setjmp.s: S, the return
address and U), signal.h (stdlib/signal.c: raised only by raise(), the
default _exit(128 + sig)), locale.h ("C" only), float.h, abort, div,
ldiv, getenv (no environment: NULL), system (no command processor),
the multibyte functions (a char each), strerror (glibc's messages),
strcoll, strxfrm, perror, fgetpos and fsetpos.  perror() and assert()
end their line with __eol, which each target's library defines: "\r"
for Color BASIC and OS-9, "\r\n" for FLEX, "\n" for the others.

Floating point in text: printf's %e, %f, %g and %a (and %E, %F, %G,
%A), and strtod, atof and scanf's %a, %e, %f and %g, are exact, as the
GNU C library's are: printf prints the exact decimal value of the double,
rounded to the nearest (to even on a tie), %a its bits in hexadecimal
(0x1.8p+1; 0x0.0000000000001p-1022 for a denormal; rounded the same way
to a precision, which may make the first digit 2, as glibc's does), and
strtod gives the double
nearest the number it reads, from big integers rather than floating
point arithmetic.  printf works out at most 340 digits (more are printed
as zeros), strtod reads 120 significant digits exactly (and the rest as
whether any isn't 0), and scanf converts the first 127 characters of a
number.  They are optional: bcc09 makes a module that calls a printf
function with a floating point argument refer to __xfpcvt, and one that
passes a pointer to a float or double to a scanf function refer to
__xfptvc (as dev86's did).  stdio/xfpcvt.s and xfptvc.s define those
symbols, and their init functions point printf and scanf at fpfmt.c and
scanfp.c; a program without them doesn't carry that code, and prints
%f as it is written, or fails to match it in scanf.  (A program that
formats a double some other way, through vsprintf in a function of its
own, say, can refer to __xfpcvt itself.)
putchar and getchar are only macros (as in dev86).

The heap
--------

malloc gets memory from sbrk() and gives it back with brk() (sbrk.s,
declared in unistd.h).  The heap starts at __end, the end of the
program's data, and grows up toward the stack, which grows down from
wherever the loader put it.  sbrk() and brk() won't take the heap within
__heap_margin bytes of the stack pointer at the time of the call (256,
unless the program changes the variable), nor below __end; then they
fail with errno ENOMEM, and malloc returns NULL.  A program that needs a
deeper stack after it has filled the heap should raise __heap_margin
first.

bcc09 doesn't understand const or volatile: features.h defines them
away when __BCC__ is defined, as bcc09 does.  Each routine that a program may need on its
own is in its own module, since ld09 only loads the members of a
library that something refers to.

Targets
-------

libc.a holds what is the same everywhere.  What differs between the
machines and systems a program can run on is in a start-up file and a
small library for each target, which bcc09 chooses with its -M option
and links with libc.a (ld09 -C<name> links crt<name>.o):

  -Ms       sa: crt0.o and libsa.a (the default)
  -Me       cb: crt0.o and libcb.a (Color BASIC, run with EXEC)
  -Mr       rom: crtrom.o and librom.a, linked with rom.ld (a Color
            BASIC ROM cartridge)
  -Mv       vec: crtvec.o and libvec.a, linked with vectrex.ld (a
            Vectrex cartridge)
  -Mi       sim: crtsim.o and libsim.a
  -Mf       flex: crtflex.o and libflex.a (a FLEX command, a .CMD file)
  -Mo       os9: crtos9.o and libos9.a (an OS-9 program module)

-Mo compiles code differently (position-independent, with the data
reached through U), so libos9.a is all of libc built again with it (in
os9obj), which bcc09 links in place of libc.a.

The others (-Mu, -Mb) have none yet, and bcc09
won't link for them (bcc09 -x leaves out the start-up code, for a
program that brings its own).  Each target's start-up code clears the
BSS and calls main; when main returns, its result goes to exit(), which
calls the atexit() functions, last registered first, and then _exit(),
which is the target's.  Before main, it calls each "init function": a
module puts the function's address in segment 5 (loc 5; 0s, which the
linker pads the segment with, are skipped).  A target's library has _exit; and read,
write, close, lseek, isatty and open if the target has a way to do I/O,
which is what stdio needs.

DP: a program's __direct variables are in segment 2, which the linker
keeps in one page, and cc1 reaches them as <name.  Before the init
functions, each target's start-up code sets DP to their page
(__seg2DL's), if there are any (__seg2DL to __seg2CH isn't empty), so a
program without them keeps the DP it was started with.  The library's
calls into the system give it the DP it wants, and the program's back
after: Color BASIC's ROM routines (POLCAT, CHROUT, JOYIN, DSKCON, and
the SOUND loop's SNDTON) DP 0; FLEX's routines the DP FLEX started the
program with (crtflex saves it in __sysdp); the Vectrex BIOS's $D0
($C8 for its music).  sa's crt0 saves the caller's DP (in __sysdp) for
_exit() to give back.  OS-9's DP is the data area's page (F$Fork's;
crtos9 sets it from U too), where segment 2 comes first: -u code reaches
__direct variables as <name too.  BASIC's interrupt handlers don't use
DP (probed in XRoar on Color, Extended, Disk 1.0 and 1.1, Super
Extended BASIC and the Dragon's ROMs), so a program may keep its own
between calls; but a C interrupt handler mustn't use __direct variables,
since DP may be 0 when it comes.

sa is for a program loaded and called by something it can return to,
such as Color BASIC's EXEC, a monitor or a debugger.  It runs on the
stack its loader gave it: crt0 saves S, and _exit() puts the status in
D, restores S and returns to the loader.  It has no I/O.

cb is for a program loaded with LOADM (or CLOADM) and run with EXEC
under Color BASIC: like sa, it runs on BASIC's stack and returns to
BASIC, with main's result in D, but it has I/O, through the ROM's POLCAT
($A000) and CHROUT ($A002) vectors (cb/rom.s).  write() prints on the
screen; read() reads a line from the keyboard as a terminal does,
showing the keys, with the left arrow to rub out and ENTER to end the
line; BREAK at the start of a line is the end of the input.  Neither
changes the bytes: a line ends with a carriage return ('\r'), on the
screen and from the keyboard, so a program prints "\r" to end a line,
and reads lines up to a '\r': getdelim(&line, &size, '\r', stdin) does
(fgets() reads to a '\n').

With Disk BASIC (on a CoCo, not a Dragon: DragonDOS's disks are
another format), open() and unlink() work on its files (cb/disk.c), and
read(), write(), lseek() and close() on the descriptors open() gives
(3 up; 4 at a time), which stdio's fopen() uses.  The files are RS-DOS's,
read and written a sector at a time through DSKCON (cb/dskcon.s), with
libc's own FAT and directory handling: names are NAME.EXT (or
NAME/EXT), with :D for drive D, or Disk BASIC's default drive; a new
file is a data file marked ASCII, as Disk BASIC's OPEN "O" makes, and
gets granules in the order Disk BASIC picks them; the FAT is written
when a file is made, closed or deleted, and a file's directory entry
when it is made and closed; write() takes a granule before it writes in
it, so on a full disk it writes what fits and then fails with ENOSPC;
lseek() can't go past the end of a file.  Disk BASIC reads the FAT
again after the program, so the two can share a disk.  A program that
opens no files doesn't carry this.  Without Disk BASIC, open() fails
with ENODEV.

coco.h (cb/coco.s, in libcb.a and librom.a) has the keyboard, the text
screen and the joysticks as BASIC has them: inkey() (INKEY$, through
POLCAT), cls(c) (CLS c, or -1 for plain CLS: fills $0400-$05FF and puts
the cursor, CURPOS at $0088, at its start), locate(row, col) (CURPOS, for
PRINT@), joystk(n) (JOYSTK, through the ROM's JOYIN vector at $A00A, from
POTVAL at $015A), sound(tone, duration) (SOUND: cb/sound.c works out
how many periods of the tone a fifteenth of a second takes, a period
being 100 + 20 * (256 - tone) cycles, and cb/tone.s plays them with
BASIC's own loop, instruction for instruction, so the pitches are
BASIC's: four writes to the DAC a period, $7E, $FE, $7E, $02, each
followed by an INCA loop up from the tone kept in BASIC's SNDTON, $8C;
the DAC is selected as the sound's source, bit 3 of $FF01 and $FF03
clear, and the sound turned on, bit 3 of $FF23, while it plays), and
peek() and poke() macros.  cls() and locate()
first call __stdio_flushout, which stdio sets (and exit.c defines), to
write out what stdout has buffered, so programs without stdio don't
carry it.  stdio flushes a line-buffered stream (stdout, on a terminal)
at a '\r' as well as a '\n', so that lines ending in '\r' show at once.

Load the program low, at $2800 or $3000 say (bcc09 -X-T0x3000), below
BASIC's stack and strings at the top of memory: the heap is the memory
between the end of the program and the stack.  "make check" tests it
with stand-ins for the ROMs (tests/src/cbrom.s, and cbdisk.s for Disk
BASIC, with a small disk in memory); "make check-xroar" tests it on the
real ROMs of the CoCo, CoCo 2, Dragon 32 and Dragon 64, in the XRoar
emulator (tests/xroar), where the Dragon's vectors turn out to be the
same, and its files on the CoCo with Disk BASIC 1.0, the CoCo 2 with
1.1 and the CoCo 3, where BASIC reads a file the program wrote and
writes one, and toolshed's decb and xrdisk.py check the disk.

rom is for a ROM cartridge, which the CoCo and the Dragon start at
$C000 (BASIC's FIRQ handler jumps there when the cartridge's CART line
is wired to Q).  bcc09 -Mr links it with the linker script rom/rom.ld
(installed as rom.ld, in the library directory), whose output is the
ROM's image: the code at $C000, then an image of the initialized data,
filled out with $FF to 8K, or to 16K less the last page; the data, BSS
and heap are in RAM from $0600, after the text screen, and the stack is
below __stack_top, $8000 (ld09 -A __stack_top=0x4000 for a 16K
machine).  crtrom (rom/crt0.s) masks the interrupts, turns off the
cartridge's FIRQ, sets S, copies the data from the ROM (__data_load) to
RAM (__data_start to __data_end), clears the BSS (__edata to __end, which
rom.ld defines), and calls the init functions and main.  librom.a is
libcb.a's I/O (but files need Disk BASIC's ROM, which the cartridge
replaces), with an _exit() (rom/_exit.s) that stops, with the status in
D, since a cartridge has nothing to return to.  The CoCo 3 starts a
cartridge before its BASIC is set up (the CHROUT vector isn't there
yet), so this I/O doesn't work on it.  "make check" runs a cartridge on
sim6809 with cbrom.s (tests/src/romtest.c); "make check-xroar" on the
CoCo, CoCo 2, Dragon 32 and Dragon 64 (tests/xroar/xrom.c).

vec is for a Vectrex cartridge.  bcc09 -Mv links it with the linker
script vec/vectrex.ld (installed as vectrex.ld), whose output is the
ROM's image: the cartridge's header (segment 12) at $0000, then
crtvec.o's code, which the BIOS runs after the header, the rest of the
code, and an image of the initialized data; the data and BSS are in the
Vectrex's RAM from $C880 (after the BIOS's variables), and the stack
below __stack_top, $CBEA.  crtvec (vec/crt0.s) sets S and DP ($D0, the
VIA's page, where the BIOS routines want it), copies the data to RAM,
clears the BSS, and calls the init functions and main.  libvec.a has
the default header (vec/header.s: "BCC09", with the BIOS's tune), which
a module of the program's own that defines __vec_header in segment 12
takes the place of (include/vectrex.h shows one); _exit() (vec/_exit.s),
which stops; and C functions for the BIOS's routines, named as Vectrex
programmers know them (vec/draw.s, lists.s, text.s, input.s, input2.s,
sound.s, sound2.s, score.s, random.s), which put their arguments in
the registers the routines want and set DP, calling them and giving the
caller's DP back after (a program's __direct variables are in page $C8).
There is no read() or write(), so no stdio.  "make check" runs a
cartridge on sim6809 with tests/src/vecbios.s standing in for the BIOS,
printing each call; "make check-vectrex" runs tests/vectrex/xvec.c in
MAME's Vectrex.

flex is for a FLEX command: "objcopy09 -O flex prog.out PROG.CMD" makes
the .CMD file, of load records and a transfer address, for any load
address in user memory (bcc09 -X-T0x1000, say).  crtflex moves the stack
from FLEX's own (128 bytes below $C080) to the end of user memory
(MEMEND, $CC2B), and gives main its arguments from FLEX's line buffer
(flex/start.c): argv[0] is the word before FLEX's line buffer pointer,
the command's name as typed, so "P,PROG", which prints PROG's output,
gives PROG; the rest are the words after it, separated by blanks or
commas.  The heap is between the end of the program and the stack.
write() sends the bytes to FLEX's PUTCHR (so P and O redirect them),
and read() reads a line from INCH, showing the characters through
PUTCHR, with TTYSET's backspace character (or ^H) to rub out and CR to
end it (shown as CR LF); a NUL at the start of a line is the end of the
input (sim6809 --flex gives NULs after its input).  Neither changes the
bytes, as on -Mo and -Me: FLEX's terminal starts a new line with CR LF
("\r\n"), and a line read ends with '\r' (getdelim(..., '\r', ...)).
_exit() goes back to FLEX (WARMS), with the status in B, which FLEX
ignores and sim6809 --flex takes as its exit status, after closing every
open file (FMSCLS).

FLEX's files (flex/file.c) go through its File Management System, with a
File Control Block from malloc for each: open(), unlink() and rename()
(stdio.h; only FLEX has rename()), and read(), write(), lseek() and
close() on the descriptors open() gives (3 up; 4 at a time).  Names are
[D.]NAME[.EXT] or NAME[.EXT][.D], made upper case.  FLEX writes a file
only from its start, into a new one: O_WRONLY needs O_TRUNC (which
deletes the old file first; fopen()'s "w" has it) if the file is there,
and O_RDWR and O_APPEND aren't possible (EINVAL).  A file is text, whose
runs of spaces FLEX stores compressed and whose NULs reading skips,
unless it is opened with O_BINARY (fcntl.h; fopen()'s "b"): then the
bytes are as they are, but read() gives the zeros that pad the last
sector, since FLEX keeps a file's size only in sectors.  lseek() works
on a file open for reading: forward, it reads up to the place (not past
the end); back, it rewinds first.  "make check" runs programs under sim6809 --flex (with arguments,
in its line buffer, and input; and FLEX's files, which its FMS keeps on
the host as their sectors' data), and "make check-flex" runs them on
Compusense FLEX for the Dragon 64 in XRoar (tests/flex), where the
files a program writes must be byte for byte the ones it writes under
sim6809.

sim is for sim6809, the simulator the tests use (the sim6809 project's,
https://github.com/retrotinker/sim6809).  crtsim
puts the stack just below the simulator's I/O block at $FF00, and
_exit() stops the simulator with the low byte of the status as its exit
status.  write() to fd 1 or 2 goes to the simulator's output, and read()
from fd 0 reads its input a line at a time, giving 0 at the end (and at
a 0 byte, which is how the simulator marks the end).  isatty() is true
for fds 0 to 2, so stdout is line buffered as on a terminal; lseek()
fails with ESPIPE; and open() fails with ENOENT, since there are no
files, so fopen() returns NULL.

os9 is for OS-9 Level 1 and 2 (and NitrOS-9) program modules: bcc09
-Mo links a program with its data at 0 (ld09 -i), and "objcopy09 -O os9
-n name prog.out name" makes the module, whose data area holds the
program's data and BSS and then the stack (objcopy09 -d sets its size,
1K if neither it nor ld09 -H does).  The code is
position-independent, and the data is reached through U, which points
at the data area.  crtos9 (os9/crt0.s) copies the data's image from the
module to the data area, clears the BSS, calls the init functions
(among them the ones cc1 makes to set static variables that hold
addresses, which aren't known until the program runs), and calls main
with argc and argv from the parameters F$Fork passed (os9/start.c:
argv[0] is the module's name, and the parameters are split at blanks,
with "..." kept together).  The heap grows from the end of the data
area F$Fork gave, which F$Mem makes bigger as it needs (os9/sbrk.c), in
whole pages; Level 1 can do that only if the memory after the data area
is free.  Memory malloc gets isn't given back to OS-9 until the program
exits, and a malloc that fails takes what it can get first (dev86's
malloc does), which on OS-9 is memory other processes could have used.
The system functions (os9/sys.c)
make OS-9's system calls (os9/call.s): read() and write() use I$ReadLn
and I$WritLn, which a terminal edits, echoes and ends with a line feed a
line at a time, so read() gives at most a line (up to a CR, OS-9's end
of line), from a file too; neither changes the bytes, so a program
writes '\r' (or "\r\n") to end a line on OS-9, and reads lines that end
with '\r' (getdelim(&line, &size, '\r', fp)); open()
uses I$Open and I$Create (O_CREAT, O_EXCL, O_TRUNC through SS.Size,
O_APPEND), lseek() I$Seek and I$GetStt's SS.Pos and SS.Size, isatty()
SS.Opt's device type (SCF), and unlink() and remove() I$Delete.
mkdir() (sys/stat.h) uses I$MakDir, with the owner's and the others'
permissions in the mode as OS-9's attributes, chdir() I$ChgDir for the
data directory, and opendir(), readdir(), rewinddir() and closedir()
(dirent.h) read RBF's 32-byte entries (".." and "." among them; d_ino
is the file descriptor's LSN) from the directory opened with DIR.
OS-9 has no calls for rmdir() and getcwd() (unistd.h), which do what
its deldir and pwd do.  rmdir() reads the directory to see that it is
empty (every entry after ".." and "." deleted: RBF would delete one that
isn't, and lose its files), turns off its D attribute with I$SetStt
SS.Attr (NitrOS-9's and Level 2's: X, the attributes; I$GetStt SS.FD
gives the old), deletes it with I$Delete, and puts the attribute back if
that fails; ENOTEMPTY if it isn't empty.  getcwd() goes up through
"..", finding each directory's name in its parent as the entry whose LSN
is the directory's own (its "." entry's), until ".." and "." are the
same, the device's root, then puts "/" and the device's name (I$GetStt
SS.DevNm) first: "/D1/USR/SRC"; ERANGE if it doesn't fit, and with a
null buffer, one from malloc.  Each call clears the registers it
doesn't set: with whatever was on the stack in them, I$MakDir once gave
a new directory nearly all of a disk.  OS-9's
errors become errno's ENOENT, EBADF, EACCES, EEXIST, ENOMEM or, for the
rest, EIO.  "make check" runs such modules on sim6809 --os9, which
emulates these calls on host files, and "make check-nitros9"
runs them on NitrOS-9 Level 2, on a CoCo 3 in XRoar (tests/nitros9).

bcc09 links programs at address 0 unless told otherwise, so give it a
load address for ld09, for example: bcc09 -o prog -X-T0x3000 prog.c

Calling conventions
-------------------

The run-time helpers for 16-bit int and unsigned operations (cc1's
softop.c) take the left operand in D and the right one in X, and leave
the result in D.  The name ends in "u" for unsigned operands.  They may
change D, X and CC, and must leave every other register as it was:
the compiler keeps values in Y and U across the call.

  imul, imulu     D * X (the low 16 bits are the same either way)
  idiv, idivu     D / X
  imod, imodu     D % X
  isl, islu       D << X
  isr             D >> X, arithmetic
  isru            D >> X, logical

The helpers for 32-bit long and unsigned long operations (cc1's
longop.c and hardop.c) hold a long in the register pair D:X, with the
high word in D and the low word in X (longs are big-endian, like the
6809).  For a binary operation, the left operand is in D:X and Y points
at the right one, high word first; the result is in D:X.  A shift
count, an int, is in Y instead.  The name ends in "u" for unsigned
operands (where it matters), then "_b" for big-endian.  They may change
D, X, Y and CC, and must leave U as it was.

  ladd_b, lsub_b                  D:X + [Y], D:X - [Y]
  land_b, lor_b, leor_b           D:X & [Y], | [Y], ^ [Y]
  lmul_b                          D:X * [Y] (low 32 bits)
  ldiv_b, ldivu_b                 D:X / [Y]
  lmod_b, lmodu_b                 D:X % [Y]
  lsl_b, lsr_b, lsru_b            D:X << Y, >> Y (arithmetic, logical)
  lneg_b, lcom_b                  -D:X, ~D:X
  lcmp_b, lcmpu_b                 set CC as a 32-bit compare of D:X
                                  with [Y] would, for signed (LT, GE,
                                  ...) or unsigned (LO, HS, ...) branches
  ltst_b                          set CC as comparing D:X with 0 would
  linc_b, ldec_b                  add 1 to, or subtract 1 from, the long
                                  at X; change only it and CC (the
                                  compiler keeps the old value in D:U)

(laddu_b, lsubu_b, landu_b, loru_b, leoru_b, lmulu_b and lslu_b are the
same routines as their signed names.)

Ordinary C functions such as memcpy take their first argument in X and
the others on the stack, in order, above the return address; the caller
removes them.  An int or pointer result is returned in X.

Variable argument lists: because the first argument comes in a
register, which the function keeps below its return address, the
address of the last named parameter doesn't lead to the variable
arguments when that is the first parameter.  So unproto (built with
_VA_ALIST_; see unproto/Makefile) puts an int parameter named __va_alist
where the "..." was in such a function's definition; it is on the stack
where the first variable argument is, and stdarg.h's va_start takes its
address.  There is no varargs.h, since va_alist is usually the first
parameter there.

Floating point is in software: floats and doubles are IEEE 754 single
and double precision, big-endian like the 6809's other numbers (the
sign and exponent first).  The helpers (bcc/fp*.s) round to the
nearest, to even on a tie, and have infinities, NaNs and denormals, as
IEEE 754 says; each result is the one a host's IEEE arithmetic gives.
The compiler does float arithmetic in double, and rounds the result to
float when it is stored (which gives the same result for +, -, * and
/).  It passes a float argument as a double, and a double argument
always on the stack (even the first); a function returns a float in
D:X (the high word in D) and a double in D, X, Y and U (the high word
in D, then X, Y, U: as PSHS D,X,U,Y leaves them in memory).  The
helpers take operands on the stack, above the return address, and at
X; they change D, X and CC, and must leave Y and U as they were:

  Fadd, Fsub, Fmul, Fdiv      pop two doubles, the left operand on top,
                              and push the result
  Faddd ... Fdivd             the right operand is the double at X:
                              the result replaces the left one
  Faddf ... Fdivf             the same, with the float at X
  Fcomp, Fcompd, Fcompf       compare (popping as above, but pushing
                              nothing) and set CC: less N and C, equal
                              Z, greater none, unordered (a NaN) C; the
                              compiler branches with BLT and BLE for <
                              and <=, BHI and BHS for > and >=, so
                              every comparison with a NaN but != is
                              false
  Ftstd, Ftstf                compare the double or float at X with 0
  Fpushd, Fpushf              push the double or float at X, as a
  Fnegd, Fnegf                double (negated)
  Fneg                        negate the double on the stack
  Fpulld, Fpullf              pop a double into the double or float at
                              X (rounding)
  dtof                        push the double at X as a float: 8 bytes,
                              the float in the first 4
  Fpushc, Fpushs, Fpushi      push the char in B, or the short or int
  Fpushuc, Fpushus, Fpushui   in D (signed, or unsigned), as a double
  Fpushl, Fpushul             ... the long in D:X
  dtoc, dtoi, dtol ...        the double at X as a char (in B), short
  ftoc, ftoi, ftol ...        or int (in D) or long (in D:X), signed or
                              unsigned (dtouc, ...), truncated; from
                              the float at X for fto...
  Fpreincd, Fpredecd          add 1 to, or subtract 1 from, the double
  Fpreincf, Fpredecf          or float at X
  Fpostincd ... Fpostdecf     the same, pushing the old value (a float
                              as dtof pushes one)

How long they take, in cycles on average, with the compiler's code
around each call (tests/fpbench.sh measures it on sim6809; a CoCo runs
895,000 cycles a second):

  double: + 1,700  * 4,300  / 8,100  < 370   to long 880, from 760
  float:  + 2,500  * 3,200  / 8,800          to double 340, from 300

Most of the time goes to the operands' records (bcc/fpcore.s): each is
unpacked to a sign, an exponent and a 64-bit mantissa, worked on, and
packed again.  Some things are done faster than that: a normal float
becomes a double, and a double whose exponent a normal float has
becomes a float, from their bits alone (__fpf2d and __fpd2f); a
multiplication leaves out the bytes of 0 at the end of either mantissa
(so floats' take 9 byte products of the 49); and a division makes 55
bits of quotient, enough for a double's 53 and a round bit, the
remainder making the sticky bit.

Behavior C leaves undefined: signed division truncates toward zero and
the remainder has the sign of the dividend (as C99 requires); division
by zero doesn't trap (see bcc/idiv.s and bcc/ldiv.s); shift counts of
the operand's size or more (16 or 32), taken as unsigned, give 0, or
copies of the sign bit for a signed right shift; a floating value
converted to an integer type it doesn't fit gives its low bits, or 0
when far out of range (or infinite, or a NaN).

The tests in tests/ check all of this on sim6809, and tests/run.sh says
what each does: among them ops.c, longops.c, fptest.c and regtest.s for
the helpers; strtest.c, stdtest.c, maltest.c and vatest.c for the
functions, also built with the host's library; crtret.c, crtexit.c and
crtatexit.c for the start-up code; pftest.c, sftest.c, siotest.c,
fltcc.c, fpio.c and mtest.c for stdio, floating point and math.h;
cbtest.c, cocotest.c, soundtest.c and cbfile.c for Color BASIC and Disk
BASIC; romtest.c for cartridges; vectest.c and veclist.c for the
Vectrex; os9io.c, os9dir.c and os9path.c for OS-9; and flexio.c and
flexfile.c for FLEX.  The make targets check-xroar, check-nitros9,
check-flex and check-vectrex run them on the real systems.
